extends ./_template_page

block content
  section.midlight
    .container-fluid
      .row.panel.panel--flush
        img.col-xs-24(src="/img/karmic/compressed/Phone_Grid_Populated.jpg")

      .row
        .default-text.default-text--round-bottom
          h1.text-center Samsara
          p.text-center Updating a legacy design system
          hr
          h2 Context
          p When Samsara approached me, their design system was badly out of date, having gone without maintenance for a number of years.
          p Their product suite had continued to expand during this time. Separate teams added competing versions of new components because cross-org visibility was limited. Components in Figma were implemented differently than mocked, and newly engineered components rarely made it back into Figma.
          p As a younger and scrappier company, it made sense to accrue technical and design debt in exchange for rapid growth. As Samsara matured, this inconsistency became costly. The now-larger company was competing for contracts in regulated sectors like state and federal government, where accessibility and UX execution were contractually required.
          p I worked between the design and engineering orgs to help Samsara repair their foundational styles and components to create a Figma library that offered ease and configurability for everyday design tasks, while remaining deeply interconnected and maintainable under the hood.
          
          hr
          //- h1.text-center Building a Roadmap
          h2 Identifying pain points
          p My first step in a new environment is to take inventory: gathering feedback, reviewing what exists, and developing an intuition for the health and importance of various areas. This takes many forms: 
          //- TODO: these styles are ugly. Need more vertical spacing and nicer bullets (dashes?)
          //- TODO: these could be a 4-up of cute sketches/illustrations
          ul
            li Shadowing the design team through key meetings, crits, and handoffs.
            li Interviewing designers about how they use the design system, what's working, and what's not.
            li Getting a dev env running and reviewing the codebase with engineers.
            li Meeting with PM and legal to understand their perspectives.
          //- h2 Results
          p As I learned more, the design system's pain points began to fall into two categories:
          .col-xs-8
            h4 Technical
            p Issues with content and functionality: absence, brokenness, or lack of parity.
          .col-xs-8
            h4 Experiential
            p Issues with using the design system: uncertainty, difficulty, and process.
          
          h2 Technical